---
import EditorialPageLayout from "../layouts/EditorialPageLayout.astro";

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How do I log something in Memoato?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Open Quick Log in Cloud mode and write what happened naturally. In Cloud mode, Memoato saves the raw note first, then processes useful categories and details in the background.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What happens to my original note?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "The original raw entry is preserved. Derived events and labels stay linked to it so corrections do not duplicate the source log.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What are categories?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Categories are views and metrics such as Weight, Pull ups, Football, or Notes. They help organize facts extracted from raw entries and can also be logged directly.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "Can I edit or delete my history?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Yes. You can edit and delete entries from category history. You can also export your data or delete your account from Profile.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What privacy options are available?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Memoato supports cloud sync, encrypted cloud, and local-only storage. Change the mode under Profile and Privacy.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "Can I connect Codex or Claude?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "In Cloud mode, create a scoped API key under Profile → API keys. Connect a remote MCP client to https://api.memoato.com/mcp using bearer authentication. Client support varies.",
      },
    },
  ],
};
const faqJsonLdString = JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd);
---

<EditorialPageLayout
  title="Help · memoato"
  description="Get started with personal memory, recall, privacy modes, MCP, and the Memoato Context pilot."
  canonicalPath="/help"
  eyebrow="Help & FAQ"
  heading="Using Memoato."
  deck="Write a first note, find it again, or connect an AI client. Project context has its own pilot entry point."
  accent="sage"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJsonLdString} />

  <nav class="help-jumps" aria-label="Help topics">
    <a href="#first-note">First note</a>
    <a href="#recall">Find a memory</a>
    <a href="#privacy-modes">Privacy</a>
    <a href="#connect-mcp">MCP setup</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="first-note">Write your first note</h2>
  <p>
    Sign in to <a href="https://app.memoato.com">Memoato</a>. In Cloud mode, open <strong>Quick Log</strong>, write what happened in your own words, and save it. Memoato saves
    the original note immediately. Useful structure can appear afterward without making you wait at capture time.
  </p>

  <h3>What can I write?</h3>
  <p>A detail you might want later. A few examples:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Lunch at Maru with Sam. Order the miso aubergine again.</code></li>
    <li><code>89.8 kg</code></li>
    <li><code>Pull ups 2 2 3</code></li>
    <li><code>Bought car battery, 59 EUR, two year warranty</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What happens to the raw note?</h3>
  <p>
    It stays intact. Memoato may derive categories, values, timestamps, and context from it, but the original source is
    preserved. Derived entries remain linked to the raw log so later corrections update the meaning without duplicating
    what you wrote.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recall">Find a memory again</h2>
  <p>In Cloud mode, open <strong>Recall</strong> and ask about a detail you saved. For example, after the lunch note above: <code>What did I want to order again at Maru?</code> Read the answer alongside its source note. If the evidence is missing or an interpretation is wrong, return to the note rather than treating the answer as certain.</p>
  <p>With an AI client, a key needs <strong>Recall only</strong> or <strong>Log and recall</strong> access. See <a href="#connect-mcp">MCP setup</a>.</p>

  <h3>What are categories?</h3>
  <p>
    Categories are useful views and metrics — Weight, Pull ups, Football, Energy, Notes, and anything personal to you.
    They are no longer the only way to begin a log. You can write naturally and let Memoato match existing categories,
    or open a category directly when you already know exactly what you want to add.
  </p>

  <h3>How does the Timeline work?</h3>
  <p>
    Timeline gives you a day-by-day view of what happened. It groups related entries, keeps notes readable, and lets you
    move backward through your history without opening every category individually.
  </p>

  <h3>Can I edit, delete, or reorder things?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Category history supports editing and deletion. Home categories can be reordered from Edit mode. Your profile
    also includes data export and account deletion controls.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy-modes">Privacy modes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cloud sync</strong> — the simplest multi-device experience.</li>
    <li><strong>Encrypted cloud</strong> — category titles and notes are encrypted on your device before saving.</li>
    <li><strong>Local-only</strong> — data stays in the browser on that device.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Open <strong>Profile → Privacy</strong> to review or switch modes. Read <a href="/privacy-first">how the modes differ</a> before switching: local-only mode deletes your account’s server data. The natural-language cloud memory and MCP flow are separate from encrypted and local category logging.</p>

  <h2 id="connect-mcp">Connect an AI client through MCP</h2>
  <p>
    Use Cloud mode and a client that supports remote MCP with bearer authentication. Support depends on the client
    and its plan; an ordinary chat window does not automatically connect to Memoato.
  </p>
  <ol class="mcp-steps">
    <li>Sign in to <a href="https://app.memoato.com/profile">Memoato → Profile → API keys</a>. Name the key for your client.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Logging only</strong>, <strong>Recall only</strong>, or <strong>Log and recall</strong>. Use the smallest access you need. <strong>Context packets only</strong> is for an already configured Context pilot workspace.</li>
    <li>Create the key and copy it once. Add <code>https://api.memoato.com/mcp</code> as a remote MCP server in your client, with <code>Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY</code>. Replace the placeholder with your key in the client’s private connection settings.</li>
    <li>Check the connection by asking the client to list its available Memoato tools. Only tools allowed by the key’s scope should appear. Review any proposed write before approving it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>You can set an expiry or revoke the key from Profile. Do not paste it into a conversation or public configuration. The client and its AI provider can see the data you ask them to handle; read <a href="/privacy-first#cloud-and-ai">how cloud memory and AI processing work</a>.</p>
  <p id="context-pilot">Project context is a separate, single-owner pilot with read-only GitHub and Linear sources. It is not an open team signup. <a href="mailto:hello@memoato.com?subject=Memoato%20Context%20pilot">Ask about a Context pilot</a>.</p>

  <h2>Still stuck?</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:hello@memoato.com">hello@memoato.com</a> or open an issue in the
    <a href="https://github.com/hrvojepavlinovic/memoato-app/issues" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub repository</a>.
  </p>
</EditorialPageLayout>

<style>
  h2[id], #context-pilot { scroll-margin-top: 7rem; }
  .help-jumps { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.25rem 1.25rem; padding-bottom: 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--editorial-sage); }
  .help-jumps a { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; }
  #first-note { margin-top: 1.75rem; padding-top: 0; border-top: 0; }
  .mcp-steps { margin: 1.5rem 0; padding-left: 1.4rem; list-style: decimal; }
  .mcp-steps li { padding-left: 0.5rem; }
  .mcp-steps li::before { content: none; }
  .mcp-steps code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
